# EmployeesNumber

EmployeesNumber is a web application for uploading employee CSV data and
visualizing each data series as an interactive line chart. It includes date
range controls, drag-and-drop upload support, upload validation, and an
Express API for parsing CSV files.

## Project Layout

The repository is split into two independent packages:

- `EmployeesNumber/` — the application under test (client + server).
- `EmployeesNumber/playwright-typescript/` — the Playwright + TypeScript end-to-end and API test framework.
- `EmployeesNumber/selenium-java/` — the Selenium + Java + TestNG + REST Assured test framework (Maven).

This separation keeps application dependencies and test-framework dependencies
independent, and the two test stacks implement the same scenarios for direct
comparison.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js and npm
- Chromium for Playwright tests
- JDK 21 and Maven for the Selenium Java tests

## Install Application Dependencies

From the project root, run:

```powershell
npm install
```

## Install Test Framework Dependencies

From the test-framework directory, run:

```powershell
cd playwright-typescript
npm install
npx playwright install chromium
```

## Start the Application

The commands to start the application are unchanged and run from the project
root.

### Production Build on `http://localhost:3000`

Build the frontend and start the Express server:

```powershell
npm run build
npm start
```

Open [http://localhost:3000] in a browser. The Express
server serves the frontend from the generated `dist` directory and exposes the
CSV upload API.

### Development Mode

Development mode uses two servers. Start each command in a separate terminal:

```powershell
# Terminal 1: Express API server
npm run server
```

```powershell
# Terminal 2: Vite development server
npm run dev
```

Open [http://localhost:5173]. Vite proxies `/api` and
`/health` requests to the Express server on port `3000` and provides hot reload.

## Run Tests

All test commands run from `playwright-typescript/`. The test framework
automatically starts the Express API server on port `3000` and the Vite server
on port `5173` before running the suite.

```powershell
cd playwright-typescript
```

Run the complete Playwright suite:

```powershell
npm test
```

Run by test layer:

```powershell
npm run test:api
npm run test:ui
npm run test:e2e
```

Run the UI tests in a visible browser:

```powershell
npm run test:ui:headed
```

Run one test file directly:

```powershell
npx playwright test tests/ui/range-selector.spec.ts
```

### Convenience Command from the Project Root

You can also run the full Playwright suite from the project root without
cd-ing into the test package:

```powershell
npm run test:playwright
```

### Type Checking

```powershell
cd playwright-typescript
npm run typecheck
```

### Generate an Allure Report

Run the tests from the test-framework directory, then generate and open the
report:

```powershell
cd playwright-typescript
npm test
npm run allure:generate
npm run allure:open
```

## Selenium Java Tests

The `selenium-java` module uses Maven, TestNG, Selenium WebDriver, and REST
Assured. It auto-starts the Express and Vite servers before the suite and
stops them afterwards.

Run the whole suite:

```powershell
cd selenium-java
mvn test
```

Or from the project root:

```powershell
npm run test:selenium
```

Useful overrides:

```powershell
mvn test -Dheadless=false          # visible browser
mvn test -Dbrowser=firefox         # firefox instead of chrome
mvn test -Dautostart.servers=false # use servers you started yourself
```
